window.GH3_DATA = window.GH3_DATA || {};

window.GH3_DATA.exam3 = {
  id: "exam3",
  name: "GH-300 \u2014 Practice Exam 3",
  timeMinutes: 100,
  units: [ { title: "", html: "", questions: [
    { n: 1, prompt: "<p>A team plans to let Copilot generate migration code for customer records containing protected personal data. What is the MOST responsible first mitigation?</p>", choices: ["Minimize and remove sensitive data from the prompt before using Copilot", "Rely on Copilot to detect all personal data during toxicity filtering", "Use a larger context window so Copilot has more complete business context", "Accept only suggestions that compile without errors"], answer: 0, topic: "Responsible AI Principles", objective: "1.1", difficulty: "easy", explanation: "<p>Minimizing sensitive data before prompting best applies privacy and security while still allowing the developer to use Copilot responsibly. The facts brief explicitly calls out personal data as a filtering category and responsible use requires mitigating foreseeable harms before the tool is invoked.</p><p>Relying on filtering is tempting because Copilot has safeguards, but filters are not a substitute for user-side data minimization. A larger context window may improve relevance but can increase exposure. Compiling code validates syntax, not privacy, fairness, or responsible AI risk.</p>" },
    { n: 2, prompt: "<p>A senior engineer accepts a Copilot-generated SQL helper after a quick visual scan. The code passes unit tests but builds SQL with string concatenation from request parameters. What should the review process require next?</p>", choices: ["Ship it because passing tests are sufficient validation for generated code", "Perform security-focused validation for injection risk before merging", "Switch the model and regenerate until the code is shorter", "Disable Copilot for the repository because one suggestion was risky"], answer: 1, topic: "Validating AI Output", objective: "1.2", difficulty: "easy", explanation: "<p>Generated code must be validated for correctness and security, not merely accepted because it looks plausible or passes a narrow test set. SQL injection is specifically named in the outbound quality and security checks, and developers still remain responsible for reviewing output.</p><p>Passing tests can be misleading when tests do not cover malicious input. Changing models does not change the need for validation or the data policy. Disabling Copilot entirely is an overreaction when the responsible action is to review, test, and correct the risky pattern.</p>" },
    { n: 3, prompt: "<p>A developer sees translucent code appear at the cursor while editing a function in VS Code. Which Copilot feature is being rendered by the IDE?</p>", choices: ["Next edit suggestions", "Agent mode", "Inline suggestions shown as ghost text", "Copilot code review"], answer: 2, topic: "Copilot in the IDE", objective: "2.1", difficulty: "easy", explanation: "<p>Inline suggestions are rendered in the IDE as ghost text at the cursor. They complete the code where the developer is currently working.</p><p>Next edit suggestions predict where the next edit should occur, not simply text at the current cursor. Agent mode is an IDE workflow for autonomous task execution. Copilot code review evaluates changes, especially pull requests, rather than rendering inline completions.</p>" },
    { n: 4, prompt: "<p>A developer wants to ask Copilot CLI for a one-off shell command from a script without entering the interactive terminal experience. Which invocation pattern best matches the supported behavior?</p>", choices: ["Install the retired gh copilot extension and run its explain command", "Open GitHub.com Copilot Chat because CLI prompts cannot be scripted", "Use Shift+Tab to cycle into plan mode, then paste the task manually", "Run GitHub Copilot CLI with a prompt argument such as -p or --prompt"], answer: 3, topic: "Copilot CLI", objective: "2.2", difficulty: "easy", explanation: "<p>GitHub Copilot CLI can run interactively or programmatically by using -p or --prompt. That is the best fit for a one-off scripted request.</p><p>The old extension wording is retired history; the current product is the standalone GitHub Copilot CLI. GitHub.com Chat is not required for CLI automation. Plan mode is real and Shift+Tab cycles modes, but manually entering plan mode is not the best scripted invocation.</p>" },
    { n: 5, prompt: "<p>An organization wants reusable internal documentation context for Copilot after the previous enterprise documentation feature was retired. Which current capability should it use?</p>", choices: ["Copilot Spaces", "Copilot Spark", "A public-code matching block list", "Next edit suggestions"], answer: 0, topic: "Copilot Features and Capabilities", objective: "2.3", difficulty: "medium", explanation: "<p>Copilot Spaces are the current replacement for retired documentation-context functionality and are the right current capability for reusable shared context.</p><p>Copilot Spark is retired and should be treated only as history. Suggestions matching public code controls whether matching public code suggestions are allowed or blocked; it does not provide documentation context. Next edit suggestions predict the location of a future edit and do not manage shared knowledge.</p>" },
    { n: 6, prompt: "<p>A user belongs to two organizations in the same enterprise. One organization allows a Copilot feature and the other restricts it. No cross-enterprise membership is involved. Which policy outcome is usually correct?</p>", choices: ["The most restrictive setting applies because policies always favor denial", "The least restrictive setting within that enterprise usually applies", "The user chooses between organization policies in personal settings", "Personal custom instructions override both policies"], answer: 1, topic: "Organization Settings and Policies", objective: "2.4", difficulty: "hard", explanation: "<p>For multiple organizations within the same enterprise, the least restrictive policy usually applies. This is a deliberate policy-precedence trap because the direction reverses across different enterprises.</p><p>The most restrictive rule is tempting because it is common in security, but the facts brief assigns that direction to different enterprises, not organizations within one enterprise. A user with organization-assigned access does not resolve this in personal settings. Custom instructions have their own precedence model and do not override administrative policies.</p>" },
    { n: 7, prompt: "<p>During Copilot prompt processing, a built prompt reaches the proxy server in a GitHub-owned Microsoft Azure tenant. Which inbound responsibility belongs to that proxy step?</p>", choices: ["Checking generated code for XSS and SQL injection after the model responds", "Retaining editor prompts for 28 days so the session can be audited", "Blocking prompt injection and jailbreak attempts before toxicity filtering", "Delivering only responses that pass output filters to the IDE"], answer: 2, topic: "Data Handling and Flow", objective: "3.1", difficulty: "hard", explanation: "<p>The inbound proxy filter blocks prompt injection and jailbreak attempts before the toxicity filter and before code generation. It is specifically not the same as content moderation.</p><p>Checking generated code for XSS and SQL injection occurs during outbound post-processing. Editor prompts are discarded after a suggestion is returned, while 28-day retention applies to Chat outside the editor, CLI, Mobile, and GitHub.com. Delivering passed responses is step 6 in the outbound path.</p>" },
    { n: 8, prompt: "<p>A study group is ordering Copilot request lifecycle steps. Which sequence correctly places the output-side validation and delivery stages?</p>", choices: ["Delivery and feedback loop, code generation, toxicity filtering", "Secure prompt transmission, proxy filter, delivery", "Code generation, secure prompt transmission, repeat for subsequent prompts", "Post-processing and response validation, suggestion delivery and feedback loop, repeat for subsequent prompts"], answer: 3, topic: "Lifecycle and Limitations", objective: "3.2", difficulty: "hard", explanation: "<p>The outbound side is step 5 post-processing and response validation, step 6 suggestion delivery and feedback loop, and step 7 repeat for subsequent prompts. This ordering matters because only responses that pass validation are delivered.</p><p>Putting delivery before code generation reverses the lifecycle. Secure prompt transmission and the proxy filter are inbound steps. Code generation is step 4, so it cannot come after secure prompt transmission in the outbound sequence.</p>" },
    { n: 9, prompt: "<p>A developer asks Copilot, \"fix this\", while no related files are open and the file name is generic. Which prompt-crafting principle is MOST directly violated?</p>", choices: ["Specific", "Few-shot", "Role prompting", "LoRA"], answer: 0, topic: "Crafting Effective Prompts", objective: "4.1", difficulty: "easy", explanation: "<p>The request lacks explicit detail about what should be fixed, so it violates Specific from the 4 Ss. A better prompt states the task, constraints, expected behavior, and relevant context.</p><p>Few-shot prompting provides examples, which may help but is not the most direct missing element. Role prompting frames Copilot as a specialist, which is optional. LoRA is a model adaptation technique, not a prompt-crafting principle.</p>" },
    { n: 10, prompt: "<p>A developer keeps a long chat history open while asking unrelated follow-up questions. Responses become less focused and consume more of the available request budget. What is the BEST prompt-engineering action?</p>", choices: ["Switch to an LTS model because LTS models ignore chat history", "Start a focused new thread or restate only the relevant context", "Add every repository file to the prompt so Copilot can choose", "Use next edit suggestions instead of Chat for all architecture questions"], answer: 1, topic: "Prompt Engineering for Performance", objective: "4.2", difficulty: "hard", explanation: "<p>Managing chat history is part of prompt engineering. Starting a focused thread or restating only relevant context reduces noise and helps Copilot use the right context for the current task.</p><p>An LTS model is a fallback when no other model is available, not a way to ignore history. Adding every file is the opposite of concise context crafting. Next edit suggestions are useful for predicting edit locations, not for every architecture discussion.</p>" }
    ,{ n: 11, prompt: "<p>A team wants Copilot to accelerate modernization of a legacy module while preserving behavior. What is the BEST use of Copilot for productivity and code quality?</p>", choices: ["Ask Copilot to rewrite the entire repository with no tests so the modernization is consistent", "Use Copilot only for comments because generated code cannot be reviewed", "Pair Copilot refactoring with existing tests and targeted human review", "Disable suggestions matching public code so suggestions are more creative"], answer: 2, topic: "Productivity and Code Quality", objective: "5.1", difficulty: "easy", explanation: "<p>Copilot can help generate, refactor, document, and modernize code, but productivity gains should be combined with tests and review to preserve behavior and quality.</p><p>Rewriting everything without tests creates unnecessary risk. Copilot can produce code, not just comments, but its output must be reviewed. Disabling public-code matching safeguards is unrelated to preserving legacy behavior and may weaken an important protection.</p>" },
    { n: 12, prompt: "<p>A CodeQL alert appears on a pull request in a repository whose maintainers do not have paid Copilot seats. Which statement about Copilot Autofix is correct?</p>", choices: ["It requires Copilot Enterprise because Autofix is an enterprise-only Copilot feature", "It requires any paid Copilot subscription before suggestions appear", "It works only after suggestions matching public code is set to Allow", "It can generate a fix suggestion for CodeQL analysis without requiring a Copilot subscription"], answer: 3, topic: "Testing and Security", objective: "5.2", difficulty: "medium", explanation: "<p>Copilot Autofix for code scanning is available for CodeQL analysis and does not require a GitHub Copilot subscription. It can generate a code change and a natural-language explanation from the alert data.</p><p>Enterprise-only and paid-subscription requirements are tempting because of the Copilot name, but the facts brief explicitly says no Copilot subscription is required. Suggestions matching public code set to Allow is not a prerequisite for Autofix.</p>" },
    { n: 13, prompt: "<p>A repository administrator configures content exclusions for a secrets fixture file in a Copilot Business repository. Which outcome should the team expect?</p>", choices: ["The excluded file will not provide content for inline suggestions, Chat responses, or Copilot code review", "The excluded file can still be directly reviewed by Copilot code review if the pull request author requests it", "The exclusion also blocks type information that the IDE provides indirectly", "The exclusion is enforced for Edit and Agent modes in all supported editors"], answer: 0, topic: "Privacy Settings and Exclusions", objective: "6.1", difficulty: "hard", explanation: "<p>Content exclusions prevent inline suggestions in affected files, prevent affected content from informing inline suggestions in other files or Copilot responses, and prevent those files from being reviewed in Copilot code review.</p><p>Allowing code review on request contradicts the exclusion behavior. IDE-provided semantic information such as type information, hover definitions, and build configuration may still be used indirectly. Content exclusion is currently not supported in Edit and Agent modes in editors, making that distractor especially tempting but wrong.</p>" },
    { n: 14, prompt: "<p>A developer with an organization-assigned Copilot seat wants to change Suggestions matching public code in personal settings. The toggle is locked. What is the BEST explanation?</p>", choices: ["The setting applies only to Xcode and NeoVim, so it is hidden elsewhere", "The setting is inherited from the organization or enterprise for organization-assigned seats", "The setting changes automatically whenever the selected model changes", "The setting checks private repositories, so individual users cannot see it"], answer: 1, topic: "Safeguards and Troubleshooting", objective: "6.2", difficulty: "hard", explanation: "<p>When a user has a seat through an organization, Suggestions matching public code is inherited from the organization or enterprise and cannot be changed in personal settings.</p><p>Xcode and NeoVim are actually not supported for code referencing. The setting applies regardless of which model is selected, so switching models does not change it. The match process searches public code on GitHub.com, not private repositories or code outside GitHub.</p>" },
    { n: 15, prompt: "<p>A Copilot-generated authentication change appears correct but the reviewer notices it omits rate limiting and lockout behavior that the existing design required. Which responsible AI limitation is MOST relevant?</p>", choices: ["Copilot cannot send prompts over HTTPS", "Suggestions matching public code always blocks authentication code", "Generative AI can misinterpret context and produce incomplete or unsafe code", "Business and Enterprise plans exclude generated code from all review"], answer: 2, topic: "Responsible AI Principles", objective: "1.1", difficulty: "medium", explanation: "<p>One named limitation is code quality and correctness, including errors, security concerns, and context misinterpretation. The generated change may look plausible while missing important requirements.</p><p>Copilot prompts are transmitted over HTTPS. Public-code matching does not always block authentication code; it checks accepted unmodified suggestions against public code when enabled. Business and Enterprise do not exempt code from review; they require responsible validation like any other plan.</p>" },
    { n: 16, prompt: "<p>A developer uses Copilot to generate tests for a payment calculation. The tests cover typical values but no boundary cases. What is the MOST responsible next step?</p>", choices: ["Trust the tests because generated tests reflect the model training corpus", "Regenerate until Copilot produces fewer test cases", "Use the tests only if Suggestions matching public code is set to Allow", "Add human-selected edge cases and assertions that reflect the payment requirements"], answer: 3, topic: "Validating AI Output", objective: "1.2", difficulty: "hard", explanation: "<p>Responsible operation requires validating AI output and using Copilot to identify edge cases and write assertions, not accepting narrow generated tests as complete. Payment behavior needs human-selected boundary cases tied to real requirements.</p><p>Training data does not guarantee coverage or correctness. Fewer tests are not a quality goal. The public-code matching setting is a safeguard for matching public code; it does not determine whether payment tests are adequate.</p>" },
    { n: 17, prompt: "<p>A developer wants Copilot to predict where the next change should happen after editing a block in VS Code. Which feature is designed for that task?</p>", choices: ["Next edit suggestions", "Ghost text", "Pull request summaries", "Copilot Spaces"], answer: 0, topic: "Copilot in the IDE", objective: "2.1", difficulty: "hard", explanation: "<p>Next edit suggestions predict the location of the developer's next edit. The facts brief distinguishes this from inline suggestions, which complete text at the current cursor.</p><p>Ghost text is how inline suggestions are rendered, so it is a tempting neighbor but not the location-prediction feature. Pull request summaries summarize changes. Copilot Spaces provide reusable context rather than predicting edit locations.</p>" },
    { n: 18, prompt: "<p>A Windows developer wants to use Copilot CLI in both PowerShell and WSL. Which platform statement is correct?</p>", choices: ["Copilot CLI runs only in Linux shells", "Copilot CLI supports Windows through PowerShell and WSL", "Copilot CLI is available only through GitHub.com Chat", "Copilot CLI requires Copilot Enterprise for terminal access"], answer: 1, topic: "Copilot CLI", objective: "2.2", difficulty: "medium", explanation: "<p>GitHub Copilot CLI runs on Linux, macOS, and Windows, including PowerShell and WSL. That makes it suitable for the developer's Windows terminal workflows.</p><p>Limiting it to Linux ignores the documented Windows support. GitHub.com Chat is a different client surface. All Copilot plans include Copilot CLI, so Enterprise is not required merely for terminal access.</p>" },
    { n: 19, prompt: "<p>A pull request uses custom review standards. The base branch has one instruction file, and the contributor's head branch changes it. Which version should Copilot code review read?</p>", choices: ["The base branch instructions, because protected branches always control review", "The organization instructions only, because they outrank repository instructions", "The head branch instructions and skills", "No instructions, because pull request reviews ignore custom instructions"], answer: 2, topic: "Copilot Features and Capabilities", objective: "2.3", difficulty: "medium", explanation: "<p>On pull requests, Copilot reads instructions and skills from the head branch, not the base branch. This is a precise source-control detail that affects code review behavior.</p><p>The base branch answer is tempting because base branches often define merge policy, but Copilot's instruction source is the head branch. Organization instructions do not outrank repository instructions in the instruction precedence model. Copilot code review can use custom instructions.</p>" },
    { n: 20, prompt: "<p>An enterprise owner wants to allow the Copilot app but block Copilot CLI for managed users. Which policy model supports this?</p>", choices: ["One client policy controls both, so the owner must allow or block both together", "Only repository administrators can configure client policies", "The setting can be changed only by each user in personal settings", "The Copilot app and Copilot CLI are governed by separate independent client policies"], answer: 3, topic: "Organization Settings and Policies", objective: "2.4", difficulty: "hard", explanation: "<p>The Copilot app and Copilot CLI have separate, independent client policies. An enterprise can allow one without allowing the other.</p><p>A single combined policy is a plausible assumption but contradicts the facts brief. Repository administrators configure some repository features such as content exclusions for their own repositories, not enterprise client policies. Managed users do not override enterprise client policy in personal settings.</p>" }
    ,{ n: 21, prompt: "<p>A compliance lead asks what is retained for Copilot use in the code editor after an inline suggestion is returned. Which answer matches the facts brief?</p>", choices: ["Prompts in the code editor are discarded once a suggestion is returned", "All editor prompts are retained for 28 days by default", "Only prompts containing personal data are retained for audit review", "Editor prompts are retained until the repository is deleted"], answer: 0, topic: "Data Handling and Flow", objective: "3.1", difficulty: "hard", explanation: "<p>For the code editor, prompts are not retained; they are discarded once a suggestion is returned. This differs from Chat used outside the editor, CLI, Mobile, and Chat on GitHub.com, where prompts, suggestions, and supporting context are typically retained for 28 days.</p><p>The 28-day value is tempting because it is a real number in the facts brief, but it has the outside-the-editor qualifier. Retention is not triggered only by personal data, and no repository-deletion retention rule is given.</p>" },
    { n: 22, prompt: "<p>A candidate memorized that Copilot Chat has an approximately 4k-token context window from the exam-source training. How should a current prep site frame that fact?</p>", choices: ["Treat it as the current product limit without qualification", "Use it as the exam-source framing and note that current product context windows are far larger", "Omit all context-window material because any mention is banned", "Turn it into a question comparing exact current model context sizes"], answer: 1, topic: "Lifecycle and Limitations", objective: "3.2", difficulty: "hard", explanation: "<p>The editorial policy says to write to the exam-source version for this point and flag that the current product has moved well beyond it. The facts brief gives approximately 200-500 lines or a few thousand tokens, and a 4k-token Chat context window, as exam-source facts.</p><p>Presenting those numbers as current reality is stale. Omitting all context-window material is unnecessary because the brief explicitly allows it with a caveat. Comparing exact current model context sizes would violate the no-specific-model-version approach and would rot quickly.</p>" },
    { n: 23, prompt: "<p>A developer asks Copilot to \"act as a security expert\" before reviewing authentication middleware. Which prompting technique is being used?</p>", choices: ["Fill-in-the-Middle preprocessing", "Few-shot prompting", "Role prompting", "LoRA adaptation"], answer: 2, topic: "Crafting Effective Prompts", objective: "4.1", difficulty: "medium", explanation: "<p>Asking Copilot to act as a security expert is role prompting. The facts brief lists role prompting examples such as security expert, performance optimization, and testing specialist.</p><p>Fill-in-the-Middle is context preprocessing around code before and after the cursor. Few-shot prompting supplies multiple examples. LoRA is a model adaptation method that adds smaller trainable parts, not a prompt style used in a chat turn.</p>" },
    { n: 24, prompt: "<p>A developer wants Copilot to build a feature through several dependent subtasks: data model, API, tests, and docs. Which interaction pattern best uses prompt process flow and chat history?</p>", choices: ["Ask one broad prompt for all implementation details and never refer to prior responses", "Keep each prompt vague so Copilot can infer the missing constraints", "Avoid chain prompting because it prevents Copilot from using context", "Use chain prompting, validating each step before using it as context for the next"], answer: 3, topic: "Prompt Engineering for Performance", objective: "4.2", difficulty: "hard", explanation: "<p>Chain prompting breaks a complex task into dependent steps while preserving useful context from earlier validated outputs. That improves performance and supports responsible validation between steps.</p><p>A single broad prompt is tempting but tends to hide errors and overload context. Vague prompts violate the 4 Ss. Chain prompting does not prevent Copilot from using context; it deliberately manages context across steps.</p>" },
    { n: 25, prompt: "<p>An engineering manager wants to quantify Copilot adoption over time using GitHub-provided metrics. Which metric is named in the facts brief?</p>", choices: ["Average Daily Active Users", "Mean time between incidents", "Pull request merge queue latency", "Number of generated comments deleted"], answer: 0, topic: "Productivity and Code Quality", objective: "5.1", difficulty: "medium", explanation: "<p>Average Daily Active Users is one of the named impact metrics, along with Total Acceptance Rate and Lines of Code Accepted. These can be sourced from the REST API for Copilot usage metrics and surveys.</p><p>Mean time between incidents and merge queue latency may be useful engineering metrics, but they are not named Copilot impact metrics in the brief. Deleted generated comments are not listed as a canonical metric.</p>" },
    { n: 26, prompt: "<p>A security engineer asks Copilot to generate tests for a deserialization routine. Which request is BEST aligned with the testing and security objective?</p>", choices: ["Generate a happy-path test only so the suite remains readable", "Generate unit tests with malformed payloads, boundary sizes, and assertions for safe failures", "Replace CodeQL analysis with Copilot-generated comments", "Set Suggestions matching public code to Allow before testing risky input"], answer: 1, topic: "Testing and Security", objective: "5.2", difficulty: "medium", explanation: "<p>Copilot can support testing and security by generating unit and integration tests, identifying edge cases, and writing assertions. Malformed payloads and safe-failure assertions match that objective directly.</p><p>Happy-path-only testing is incomplete for a security-sensitive parser. Copilot comments do not replace code scanning. The public-code matching setting is unrelated to whether deserialization tests exercise risky inputs.</p>" },
    { n: 27, prompt: "<p>A repository contains a symlink to a directory with excluded files. The team expects content exclusions to cover it. What should the administrator know?</p>", choices: ["Symlinks are covered only when Suggestions matching public code is set to Block", "Symlinks are covered only on Copilot Enterprise", "Content exclusions do not apply to symbolic links", "Symlinks are covered in Edit mode but not inline suggestions"], answer: 2, topic: "Privacy Settings and Exclusions", objective: "6.1", difficulty: "hard", explanation: "<p>Content exclusions do not apply to symbolic links. That limitation is explicitly called out in the facts brief and is a high-value boundary case.</p><p>Blocking public-code matches does not extend exclusions to symlinks. Enterprise ownership can configure exclusions, but the symlink limitation remains. Edit mode is not a supported enforcement area for content exclusion in editors, so that distinction does not rescue the symlink scenario.</p>" },
    { n: 28, prompt: "<p>A developer accepts an unmodified Copilot suggestion. Later, a teammate asks whether their own manually written code is checked by the same public-code matching process. What is correct?</p>", choices: ["All code in the file is checked, whether written by Copilot or a human", "Only private repository code is checked, because public code is ignored", "Altered Copilot suggestions are always checked more strictly than unmodified ones", "Only accepted, unmodified Copilot suggestions are checked for matches"], answer: 3, topic: "Safeguards and Troubleshooting", objective: "6.2", difficulty: "hard", explanation: "<p>Suggestions matching public code checks accepted, unmodified Copilot suggestions. Code a developer wrote and Copilot suggestions that were altered are not checked for matches by that process.</p><p>Checking every line in a file is broader than the documented behavior. The index searches public repositories on GitHub.com, not private code. Altered Copilot suggestions are specifically excluded from this match process.</p>" },
    { n: 29, prompt: "<p>A developer wants to publish a generated change immediately because Copilot produced a confident explanation. Which responsible AI principle assigns ownership for deciding whether that output is acceptable?</p>", choices: ["Accountability", "Inclusiveness", "Auto model selection", "Fill-in-the-Middle"], answer: 0, topic: "Responsible AI Principles", objective: "1.1", difficulty: "hard", explanation: "<p>Accountability means people and organizations remain responsible for AI-assisted outcomes. A confident explanation does not transfer responsibility from the developer or team to Copilot.</p><p>Inclusiveness is about access and equitable participation, not ownership of decisions. Auto model selection is a model-access concept. Fill-in-the-Middle is prompt preprocessing, not a responsible AI governance principle.</p>" },
    { n: 30, prompt: "<p>A team is validating a Copilot-generated accessibility change. It compiles and passes snapshots, but no screen-reader behavior has been checked. What is the BEST next action?</p>", choices: ["Merge because compiled UI code is validated", "Test the generated change against the intended accessibility behavior and user impact", "Assume inclusiveness is satisfied because Copilot suggested the code", "Rely on public-code matching to validate accessibility"], answer: 1, topic: "Validating AI Output", objective: "1.2", difficulty: "medium", explanation: "<p>Validating AI output means checking the generated result against the real requirement and user impact. For accessibility, that includes behavior for assistive technologies, not just compilation and snapshots.</p><p>Compiling is necessary but incomplete. Copilot suggesting code does not guarantee inclusiveness. Public-code matching is a safeguard for matching public code and does not validate accessibility behavior.</p>" }
    ,{ n: 31, prompt: "<p>A team uses NeoVim and asks why Agent mode is not available even though inline suggestions work. Which distinction explains the behavior?</p>", choices: ["Agent mode is available only when Suggestions matching public code is set to Allow", "Inline suggestions and Agent mode are the same feature, so the IDE is misconfigured", "Inline suggestions support NeoVim, but Agent mode is not available in NeoVim", "Agent mode requires GitHub Enterprise Server"], answer: 2, topic: "Copilot in the IDE", objective: "2.1", difficulty: "hard", explanation: "<p>The support matrix distinguishes inline suggestions from Agent mode. Inline suggestions are supported in Vim/NeoVim, while Agent mode is not available in NeoVim.</p><p>The public-code matching setting does not enable Agent mode. Inline suggestions and Agent mode are different feature categories. Copilot is not currently available for GitHub Enterprise Server, so that platform cannot be the requirement.</p>" },
    { n: 32, prompt: "<p>A teammate says Copilot CLI plan mode can be used only in a graphical IDE. What correction is most accurate?</p>", choices: ["Plan mode is only a GitHub.com pull request review feature", "Plan mode was part of a retired GitHub App-based extension and no longer exists", "Plan mode requires Copilot Enterprise and cannot run locally", "Copilot CLI includes plan mode, and Shift+Tab cycles modes in the terminal"], answer: 3, topic: "Copilot CLI", objective: "2.2", difficulty: "medium", explanation: "<p>GitHub Copilot CLI has a plan mode, and Shift+Tab cycles modes. It is a terminal feature that can run interactively in supported operating systems and shells.</p><p>Plan mode is not limited to pull request review. It is not merely a retired extension capability. All plans include Copilot CLI, so Enterprise is not required for local terminal use.</p>" },
    { n: 33, prompt: "<p>A developer changes the model picker for Chat and expects inline suggestions and next edit suggestions to use the same selection automatically. What should they know?</p>", choices: ["Inline suggestions, next edit suggestions, and Chat have independent model selections", "Changing Chat always changes every Copilot model selection", "Model selection changes the data collection and usage policy", "The model picker is unavailable outside Copilot Free and Student"], answer: 0, topic: "Copilot Features and Capabilities", objective: "2.3", difficulty: "medium", explanation: "<p>Inline suggestions, next edit suggestions, and Chat are three independent model selections. Changing one does not change the others.</p><p>The automatic-all-surfaces assumption is a common trap. The facts brief also states that changing the AI model does not change data collection and usage policy. Free and Student have auto model selection only, while other plans can have model selections depending on policy.</p>" },
    { n: 34, prompt: "<p>A user belongs to two different enterprises. One enterprise disables Copilot Chat in GitHub and the other allows it. Which policy result is expected?</p>", choices: ["The least restrictive policy applies because at least one enterprise allows the feature", "The most restrictive policy almost always applies across different enterprises", "The user's personal instructions decide because they have highest precedence", "The organization with the most recent policy update wins"], answer: 1, topic: "Organization Settings and Policies", objective: "2.4", difficulty: "hard", explanation: "<p>Across different enterprises, the most restrictive policy almost always applies. The facts brief gives Copilot Chat in GitHub as the example: if any enterprise disables it, it is disabled for that user.</p><p>The least-restrictive rule is tempting because it applies within a single enterprise across multiple organizations. Personal instructions have high precedence for instructions, not policies. Recency is not the conflict-resolution rule.</p>" },
    { n: 35, prompt: "<p>A candidate maps lifecycle steps and labels toxicity filtering as only an outbound process. What is the best correction?</p>", choices: ["Toxicity filtering happens only after delivery as part of feedback", "Toxicity filtering is the same as the inbound proxy jailbreak filter", "Toxicity filtering runs inbound before intent extraction and again during outbound validation", "Toxicity filtering applies only when a paid plan uses premium models"], answer: 2, topic: "Data Handling and Flow", objective: "3.1", difficulty: "hard", explanation: "<p>The toxicity filter runs on both legs: inbound before intent extraction, and outbound again during post-processing and response validation. The facts brief names hate speech, inappropriate content, and personal data as inbound categories.</p><p>It does not happen after delivery; only passed responses are delivered. It is distinct from the proxy jailbreak filter. It is not tied to paid plans or premium model access.</p>" },
    { n: 36, prompt: "<p>A Copilot suggestion fails an outbound quality check for a common web vulnerability. What happens according to the lifecycle description?</p>", choices: ["It is delivered with a warning so the developer can decide", "It is retained for 180 days in the audit log", "It bypasses filtering if the repository has content exclusions", "It is truncated or discarded before delivery"], answer: 3, topic: "Lifecycle and Limitations", objective: "3.2", difficulty: "hard", explanation: "<p>During outbound post-processing, the proxy applies code quality checks for common bugs or vulnerabilities such as XSS and SQL injection. If any part of the response fails checks, it is truncated or discarded.</p><p>The lifecycle does not say failed output is delivered with a warning. Audit logs do not include local client session data such as prompts, and retention is a different topic. Content exclusions do not make unsafe output bypass validation.</p>" },
    { n: 37, prompt: "<p>A developer wants Copilot to infer a testing pattern from two examples before writing another test. Which prompt type best describes this?</p>", choices: ["Few-shot prompting", "Zero-shot prompting", "Auto model selection", "Prompt file retirement"], answer: 0, topic: "Crafting Effective Prompts", objective: "4.1", difficulty: "medium", explanation: "<p>Few-shot prompting provides multiple examples so Copilot can infer the desired pattern before generating a new output. The study guide names zero-shot and few-shot, and the training also defines one-shot.</p><p>Zero-shot prompting gives no examples. Auto model selection controls model choice, not example-based prompting. Prompt files are a supported reuse mechanism in some IDEs; they are not retired and do not describe this example pattern.</p>" },
    { n: 38, prompt: "<p>A repository has generic file names, unrelated tabs open, and missing framework context. The developer asks why Copilot's answer seems off. Which performance principle best explains the issue?</p>", choices: ["Copilot never uses file paths or open tabs as context", "Surround matters: descriptive filenames and related open files help provide useful context", "Only organization instructions can provide context to Copilot", "Changing Suggestions matching public code to Block removes the need for context"], answer: 1, topic: "Prompt Engineering for Performance", objective: "4.2", difficulty: "hard", explanation: "<p>Surround, one of the 4 Ss, means using descriptive filenames and keeping related files open. The lifecycle also lists filename, file type, adjacent open tabs, project structure, languages, and frameworks as gathered context.</p><p>Saying Copilot never uses those signals contradicts the process flow. Organization instructions can help, but they are not the only context. Blocking public-code matches is a safeguard, not a replacement for relevant project context.</p>" },
    { n: 39, prompt: "<p>A manager wants a staged Copilot rollout metric program. Which sequence matches the facts brief?</p>", choices: ["Optimization, Evaluation, Sustained efficiency, Adoption", "Adoption, Evaluation, Optimization, Sustained efficiency", "Evaluation, Adoption, Optimization, Sustained efficiency", "Sustained efficiency, Adoption, Evaluation, Optimization"], answer: 2, topic: "Productivity and Code Quality", objective: "5.1", difficulty: "medium", explanation: "<p>The four stages for measuring impact are Evaluation, Adoption, Optimization, and Sustained efficiency. This sequence helps an organization move from baseline measurement through durable productivity gains.</p><p>The other sequences use real stage names, which makes them plausible, but they scramble the order given in the facts brief. Optimization before evaluation or sustained efficiency before adoption would make the program harder to interpret.</p>" },
    { n: 40, prompt: "<p>A CodeQL Autofix suggestion appears on a pull request. What inputs support the generated fix?</p>", choices: ["The user's full local Copilot CLI prompt history", "A secret-scanning token dump and the organization's billing data", "Only the file name and selected model", "CodeQL alert data in SARIF format, surrounding code snippets, and query help text"], answer: 3, topic: "Testing and Security", objective: "5.2", difficulty: "medium", explanation: "<p>Copilot Autofix uses CodeQL alert data in SARIF format, surrounding code snippets, and query help text. Each suggestion includes the code change and a natural-language explanation.</p><p>Local Copilot CLI prompt history is not an Autofix input and is not included in audit logs. Secret data and billing data are not the stated inputs. File name and selected model alone would be insufficient for a CodeQL-based fix.</p>" }
    ,{ n: 41, prompt: "<p>A Pro+ subscriber asks an organization owner to enable content exclusions for a personal repository outside any Business or Enterprise organization. What is the correct boundary?</p>", choices: ["Content exclusion is available only with Copilot Business and Copilot Enterprise", "Content exclusion is available on every paid individual plan", "Content exclusion is available on Copilot Free but only for public repositories", "Content exclusion is replaced by the model picker on individual plans"], answer: 0, topic: "Privacy Settings and Exclusions", objective: "6.1", difficulty: "hard", explanation: "<p>Content exclusion is a Copilot Business and Copilot Enterprise feature. Individual paid plans such as Pro, Pro+, and Max do not unlock it according to the facts brief.</p><p>Assuming any paid plan qualifies is tempting because Pro+ and Max are higher individual tiers, but the feature boundary is organizational. Copilot Free does not add repository content exclusion. The model picker controls model selection, not file exclusion policy.</p>" },
    { n: 42, prompt: "<p>An enterprise enables Suggestions matching public code and a developer selects a different model for Chat. What happens to the safeguard?</p>", choices: ["It turns off until the developer restarts the IDE", "It applies regardless of which model is selected", "It applies only to models from GitHub-owned providers", "It checks only code outside GitHub.com after a model switch"], answer: 1, topic: "Safeguards and Troubleshooting", objective: "6.2", difficulty: "hard", explanation: "<p>Content filters, including public-code matching when enabled, apply regardless of which model is chosen. The facts brief also says changing the model does not change data collection and usage policy.</p><p>Restarting the IDE is not the rule. Provider identity is not the condition stated for the safeguard. The match process searches public repositories on GitHub.com, not code outside GitHub.com.</p>" },
    { n: 43, prompt: "<p>A team plans to let Copilot produce user-facing eligibility logic. They worry one demographic group may receive systematically different results. Which responsible AI principle is most directly implicated?</p>", choices: ["Transparency", "LoRA", "Fairness", "Prompt history management"], answer: 2, topic: "Responsible AI Principles", objective: "1.1", difficulty: "hard", explanation: "<p>Fairness addresses biased or inequitable outcomes across groups. Eligibility logic that systematically disadvantages a demographic group is a direct fairness risk.</p><p>Transparency helps people understand AI behavior, but it is not the principle most directly tied to demographic disparity. LoRA is an adaptation technique. Prompt history management can improve relevance but does not define the ethical harm.</p>" },
    { n: 44, prompt: "<p>A generated license header resembles a public project. The developer edits several lines before accepting it. Which statement best describes public-code matching coverage?</p>", choices: ["The edited suggestion is still checked because all accepted code is scanned", "The edited suggestion is checked only if it is over the approximate comparison window", "The edited suggestion is checked only in NeoVim", "Suggestions the user alters are not checked by the matching process"], answer: 3, topic: "Validating AI Output", objective: "1.2", difficulty: "medium", explanation: "<p>The matching process checks accepted, unmodified Copilot suggestions. Copilot suggestions the user has altered are not checked for matches by that feature, so developers must still validate provenance and suitability.</p><p>All accepted code being scanned sounds safer but is broader than the documented behavior. The approximate comparison window should not be used as a decisive exam distinction. NeoVim is not supported for code referencing.</p>" },
    { n: 45, prompt: "<p>A developer in Visual Studio asks why Edit mode is missing even though Chat works. Which support statement is correct?</p>", choices: ["Edit mode is supported in VS Code and JetBrains, not Visual Studio", "Edit mode is supported only in Visual Studio", "Edit mode is supported only on GitHub.com", "Edit mode was retired and replaced by pull request summaries"], answer: 0, topic: "Copilot in the IDE", objective: "2.1", difficulty: "medium", explanation: "<p>Edit mode is supported in VS Code and JetBrains and is not supported in Visual Studio, Eclipse, or Xcode. This is a current-name feature boundary.</p><p>Visual Studio support is a tempting assumption because other Copilot features work there, but this mode does not. GitHub.com is not the support surface for prompt files or this editor mode. Edit mode is current; it is the current name for a feature previously called Copilot Edits in older material.</p>" },
    { n: 46, prompt: "<p>A team wants a repository-specific reusable prompt for a release-note draft in VS Code. Where should the prompt file live?</p>", choices: [".github/hooks/release.prompt.json", ".github/prompts/release-notes.prompt.md", "AGENTS.md under the repository root", "The enterprise AI controls tab"], answer: 1, topic: "Copilot Features and Capabilities", objective: "2.3", difficulty: "medium", explanation: "<p>Prompt files live at .github/prompts/*.prompt.md. They are generally available in VS Code and Visual Studio, with preview support in JetBrains and Xcode.</p><p>Hooks live under .github/hooks/*.json and are not prompt files. AGENTS.md provides agent instructions, not reusable prompt-file content. Enterprise AI controls manage policy rather than storing repository prompt templates.</p>" },
    { n: 47, prompt: "<p>A developer asks what context Copilot gathers at the first lifecycle step for an inline suggestion. Which set best matches the facts brief?</p>", choices: ["Only the current line and the user's billing plan", "Only pull request comments and audit-log records", "Code before and after the cursor, file metadata, adjacent tabs, project structure, languages, frameworks, and FIM preprocessing", "Only public GitHub repositories and private repository matches"], answer: 2, topic: "Data Handling and Flow", objective: "3.1", difficulty: "hard", explanation: "<p>Step 1 gathers code before and after the cursor, filename and file type, adjacent open tabs, project structure and file paths, languages and frameworks, and uses Fill-in-the-Middle preprocessing. The prompt is also transmitted securely over HTTPS.</p><p>The current line alone is far too narrow. Pull request comments and audit logs are not the lifecycle context list. Public-code matching is an optional outbound safeguard, not the initial prompt-context set.</p>" },
    { n: 48, prompt: "<p>A developer gives Copilot one complete example of a desired parser test, then asks it to generate a similar test for another input. Which training-defined prompt category fits best?</p>", choices: ["Zero-shot", "Few-shot", "Role prompting", "One-shot"], answer: 3, topic: "Crafting Effective Prompts", objective: "4.1", difficulty: "medium", explanation: "<p>One-shot prompting uses one example to guide the response. The study guide names zero-shot and few-shot, but the training also defines one-shot, so it is fair game.</p><p>Zero-shot uses no examples. Few-shot uses multiple examples, which makes it a tempting nearby term but not the best fit for exactly one example. Role prompting asks Copilot to act in a role such as security expert or testing specialist.</p>" },
    { n: 49, prompt: "<p>An organization reviews generated code for a medical scheduling tool. The output works for common cases but fails for users relying on assistive technology. Which responsible AI principle did the team most directly neglect?</p>", choices: ["Inclusiveness", "Suggestions matching public code", "Long-term support model fallback", "Agent mode"], answer: 0, topic: "Responsible AI Principles", objective: "1.1", difficulty: "hard", explanation: "<p>Inclusiveness requires that systems work for people with different needs and abilities. A tool that fails users relying on assistive technology creates an inclusiveness issue even when common cases pass.</p><p>Suggestions matching public code is an IP and provenance safeguard, not an accessibility principle. LTS fallback is a model availability concept. Agent mode is an IDE workflow, not the ethical principle at issue.</p>" },
    { n: 50, prompt: "<p>A developer's prompt says, \"Create API docs, refactor the cache, explain OAuth, generate fixtures, and review the PR.\" Which 4 Ss principle should be applied first?</p>", choices: ["Surround", "Single", "Short", "Specific"], answer: 1, topic: "Prompt Engineering for Performance", objective: "4.2", difficulty: "medium", explanation: "<p>Single means focusing a prompt on one well-defined task or question. The example bundles several unrelated tasks, so splitting it is the first performance improvement.</p><p>Surround helps with context such as related files and descriptive names. Short and Specific also matter, but the most immediate violation is that the prompt is not a single focused task.</p>" }
    ,{ n: 51, prompt: "<p>A developer validates Copilot output by checking only whether it compiles. The feature touches authorization and error handling. Which missing validation is most important?</p>", choices: ["Checking whether the suggestion came from the current model picker", "Confirming that the code is longer than the surrounding context", "Reviewing authorization behavior, security impact, and edge cases against requirements", "Making sure the prompt was sent from GitHub.com instead of an IDE"], answer: 2, topic: "Validating AI Output", objective: "1.2", difficulty: "medium", explanation: "<p>Responsible validation must assess behavior, security impact, and edge cases against the real requirements. Compiling is not enough for authorization code because plausible generated code can still be unsafe or incomplete.</p><p>The model picker does not determine correctness. Code length relative to context is not a validation standard. The client surface used for the prompt does not remove the need to review generated authorization behavior.</p>" },
    { n: 52, prompt: "<p>A developer is learning an unfamiliar framework and wants to reduce context switching. Which Copilot use best fits the productivity objective while remaining responsible?</p>", choices: ["Ask Copilot to disable tests until the framework is learned", "Paste confidential production records so Copilot can infer realistic examples", "Assume generated examples are authoritative documentation", "Ask Copilot for framework-specific examples, then verify them against project constraints and docs"], answer: 3, topic: "Productivity and Code Quality", objective: "5.1", difficulty: "medium", explanation: "<p>Copilot can accelerate learning and reduce context switching by producing examples and explanations, but those outputs should be verified against the project's constraints and authoritative documentation.</p><p>Disabling tests reduces quality. Pasting confidential production records violates responsible data handling. Generated examples are useful but not authoritative merely because Copilot produced them.</p>" },
    { n: 53, prompt: "<p>A developer wants to bring their own model key for Copilot in an editor. Which support statement is correct?</p>", choices: ["BYOK is generally available in Visual Studio, preview in VS Code, JetBrains, Eclipse, and Xcode, and not supported in NeoVim", "BYOK is supported only in NeoVim", "BYOK changes Copilot data collection policy for that user", "BYOK is available only on GitHub Enterprise Server"], answer: 0, topic: "Copilot in the IDE", objective: "2.1", difficulty: "medium", explanation: "<p>Bring your own key is generally available in Visual Studio, preview in VS Code, JetBrains, Eclipse, and Xcode, and not supported in NeoVim. That is the durable support boundary in the facts brief.</p><p>NeoVim-only support is the opposite of the stated boundary. Changing model or key choices does not change the data collection and usage policy. Copilot is not currently available for GitHub Enterprise Server.</p>" },
    { n: 54, prompt: "<p>A team uses Copilot to suggest performance improvements to a hot path. Which validation approach best supports testing and security?</p>", choices: ["Merge the suggestion if it makes the code shorter", "Benchmark the change, run targeted tests, and review for security regressions", "Prefer suggestions that match public code exactly", "Skip human review because performance changes are low risk"], answer: 1, topic: "Testing and Security", objective: "5.2", difficulty: "medium", explanation: "<p>Copilot can suggest performance optimizations, but the team should benchmark the change, run targeted tests, and review for security regressions. This validates both performance and safety.</p><p>Shorter code is not automatically faster or safer. Exact public-code matches are not a goal, and matching safeguards may block unmodified matches when configured. Performance changes can introduce subtle correctness or security issues, so human review remains necessary.</p>" },
    { n: 55, prompt: "<p>A candidate says Fill-in-the-Middle is an outbound check that blocks public-code matches. What is the best correction?</p>", choices: ["It is the audit-log query for Copilot events", "It is an organization policy conflict rule", "It is inbound preprocessing that considers both preceding and following code", "It is the final delivery step after response validation"], answer: 2, topic: "Data Handling and Flow", objective: "3.1", difficulty: "hard", explanation: "<p>Fill-in-the-Middle is an inbound preprocessing technique that considers code before and after the cursor. It helps turn the user's request and context into a concrete coding task before model generation.</p><p>Audit-log queries use filters such as action:copilot, not FIM. Policy conflict rules concern enterprise and organization settings. Final delivery is step 6, after outbound validation, and is unrelated to FIM.</p>" },
    { n: 56, prompt: "<p>An organization excludes a file and then notices Copilot still seems aware of a type name from that file through IDE hover data. What is the correct explanation?</p>", choices: ["The exclusion failed because excluded files are always used by Chat", "The repository URL was logged and replayed into the prompt", "Content exclusion applies only to Copilot Free", "Copilot may use semantic information provided indirectly by the IDE, such as type or hover information"], answer: 3, topic: "Privacy Settings and Exclusions", objective: "6.1", difficulty: "medium", explanation: "<p>Content exclusions do not stop Copilot from using semantic information if the IDE provides it indirectly, such as type information, hover-over definitions, or build configuration. This is a documented limitation.</p><p>Excluded file content should not inform Chat responses, so the behavior does not mean exclusions always fail. Only the repository URL is sent to retrieve policy, and those URLs are not logged anywhere. Content exclusion is for Business and Enterprise, not Free.</p>" },
    { n: 57, prompt: "<p>A manager wants to evaluate whether a Copilot deployment is safe by looking only at acceptance rate. Which responsible AI concern is missing?</p>", choices: ["High adoption metrics do not prove correctness, fairness, security, or accountability", "Acceptance rate is not a named Copilot metric", "Responsible AI applies only to generated prose, not code", "If users accept suggestions, validation is no longer needed"], answer: 0, topic: "Responsible AI Principles", objective: "1.1", difficulty: "medium", explanation: "<p>Acceptance metrics can help measure usage plus productivity, but they do not prove that generated code is correct, fair, secure, inclusive, transparent, or governed accountably. Responsible AI requires more than usage measurement.</p><p>Total Acceptance Rate is a named metric, so dismissing it entirely is wrong. Responsible AI applies to code and developer workflows, not just prose. User acceptance does not replace validation.</p>" },
    { n: 58, prompt: "<p>A developer asks which Copilot plans include GitHub Copilot CLI. Which answer is correct?</p>", choices: ["Only Copilot Business and Copilot Enterprise include the CLI", "All plans include Copilot CLI", "Only Copilot Free excludes the CLI", "Only plans with priority premium model access include the CLI"], answer: 1, topic: "Copilot CLI", objective: "2.2", difficulty: "medium", explanation: "<p>All Copilot plans include Copilot CLI and the Copilot app. This includes individual, organizational, and enterprise plans.</p><p>Business and Enterprise are not the only plans with CLI access. Copilot Free includes the CLI. Priority premium model access is a model entitlement boundary, not a CLI availability boundary.</p>" },
    { n: 59, prompt: "<p>A product owner asks for the current replacement path after Copilot Spark retired. Which recommendation matches the facts brief?</p>", choices: ["Create new Spark apps because existing retirement notices apply only to mobile clients", "Use GitHub Models directly because it remains the live inference service behind Spark", "Build directly with Copilot in VS Code, Copilot CLI, and the GitHub Copilot app", "Move the work to Copilot Workspace because it remains the preferred replacement"], answer: 2, topic: "Copilot Features and Capabilities", objective: "2.3", difficulty: "medium", explanation: "<p>Copilot Spark is retired, and the replacement guidance is to build directly with Copilot in VS Code, Copilot CLI, and the GitHub Copilot app. Existing deployed apps continuing to work does not make Spark a live authoring path.</p><p>New Spark app creation is not available after retirement. GitHub Models, the inference service behind Spark's llm() function, is retired. Copilot Workspace is also retired and should not be presented as the current replacement.</p>" },
    { n: 60, prompt: "<p>An organization wants IP indemnity protection for Copilot suggestions under the confirmed GitHub terms. Which configuration is required?</p>", choices: ["Any individual plan with Suggestions matching public code set to Allow", "Copilot Free with code referencing disabled", "Copilot Business or Enterprise with Suggestions matching public code set to Allow", "Copilot Business or Enterprise with Suggestions matching public code set to Block"], answer: 3, topic: "Safeguards and Troubleshooting", objective: "6.2", difficulty: "medium", explanation: "<p>IP indemnity applies to Copilot Business and Copilot Enterprise, and GitHub assumes legal responsibility only when Suggestions matching public code is set to Block. Both conditions are required in the confirmed facts brief.</p><p>Individual plans are not the indemnity boundary. Copilot Free does not provide this protection. Setting Suggestions matching public code to Allow fails the required condition even on Business or Enterprise.</p>" }
  ] } ]
};

